--- !u!114 &63723635
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 63723630}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 4e477467904f32d0fbd54614fe8e023d, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MissionBriefing
  ascendCADData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 43cbf5acf3c2c65fa8ec5b18870623d0, type: 2}
  senderName: Mission Control
  elevatorDoorBox: {fileID: 63723632}
  elevatorButtonBox: {fileID: 1373354924}
  interactDistance: 4
  elevatorKey: 101
  player: {fileID: 1322462854}
  teleportIntoElevator: 1
  elevatorInsidePoint: {fileID: 0}
  elevatorInsidePosition: {x: 810.2, y: 2, z: 710}
  setElevatorFacing: 1
  elevatorFacingY: -90
  meetingRoomPoint: {fileID: 0}
  meetingRoomPosition: {x: 811, y: 1.08, z: 721.2}
  setMeetingRoomFacing: 1
  meetingRoomFacingY: -90
  levelCameraOnTeleport: 1
  disableWhileBusy:
  - {fileID: 1322462855}
  hideWhileBusy: []
  closePhoneKey: 112
  skipKey: 115
  continueKey: 32
  font: {fileID: 0}
  textScale: 1
  speakerName: Mission Director
  welcomeEmail: 'Dear Astronaut,


    Welcome to the ASCEND Space Agency! We are very happy to have you on our team.


    Our scientists have three missions that need a brave astronaut like you. Each one will teach us something new about our planet and our Moon.


    Please read the missions below and tap the one you would like to lead.


    Kind regards,

    Mission Control

    ASCEND Space Agency'
  lettersPerSecond: 45
  floorMessageSeconds: 1.5
  fadeSeconds: 0.6
  missions:
  - title: Sky Sensor (CanSat)
    subtitle: Easy  |  Measure the air while falling from the sky
    briefingLines:
    - 'Welcome to the Briefing Room, astronaut! Today you will learn about your mission: Sky Sensor.'
    - Your job is to build a CanSat. A CanSat is a tiny satellite, about the size of a soda can!
    - A small rocket will carry your CanSat high into the sky, about one kilometre up. That is higher than the tallest building in the world!
    - At the top, your CanSat pops out of the rocket and opens its parachute.
    - 'While it floats down, its sensors measure the air: how warm it is, and how hard the air is pushing. We call that air pressure.'
    - 'Fun fact: the higher you go, the colder the air gets and the thinner it becomes.'
    - Your CanSat uses a little radio to send its measurements to our ground station, so scientists can read them right away.
    - Be careful when you build it! If the parachute is too small, your CanSat will fall too fast and might break when it lands.
    - Real scientists send up balloons and small probes like this every day to help forecast the weather.
    - First, you will design your CanSat and choose its parts. Good luck, astronaut!
    summaryEmail: 'Mission: Sky Sensor (CanSat)

      Difficulty: Easy


      YOUR GOALS

      - Launch your CanSat about 1 km high

      - Open the parachute

      - Measure air temperature and air pressure while falling

      - Send your data to the ground station by radio

      - Land softly without breaking


      WHAT YOU WILL BUILD

      Rocket: a small rocket that can lift a can-sized payload

      Payload: your CanSat (about the size of a soda can)

      Science instruments: temperature sensor, air pressure sensor

      Other parts: parachute, battery, small radio


      TIPS

      - A bigger parachute means a softer landing, but it adds weight.

      - Keep your CanSat light so the rocket can lift it easily.


      Next step: design your mission. Good luck!

      Mission Control'
  - title: Earth Watcher
    subtitle: Medium  |  Take pictures of Earth from orbit
    briefingLines:
    - Welcome, astronaut! Your mission is called Earth Watcher.
    - You will send a satellite into orbit around Earth. Orbit means it keeps falling around the planet without ever hitting the ground!
    - Your satellite will fly about 700 kilometres above Earth. That is way higher than any airplane can go.
    - 'To stay in orbit it must move super fast: about 27,000 kilometres per hour!'
    - Your satellite carries a special camera. It can see colours our eyes can't see, which helps scientists check if forests and farms are healthy.
    - Its solar panels turn sunlight into electricity, and its antenna sends the pictures back to Earth.
    - You will need a bigger rocket this time, because getting to orbit takes a lot of energy.
    - NASA has a real mission just like this called Landsat. Landsat satellites have been taking pictures of Earth since 1972!
    - 'Remember: every part adds weight, and a heavier satellite needs a bigger, more expensive rocket.'
    - Now let's go design your satellite. Good luck, astronaut!
    summaryEmail: 'Mission: Earth Watcher

      Difficulty: Medium


      YOUR GOALS

      - Launch your satellite into orbit, about 700 km above Earth

      - Open the solar panels to get power

      - Take pictures of Earth''s land, forests and farms

      - Send the pictures to the ground station


      WHAT YOU WILL BUILD

      Rocket: a medium rocket that can reach orbit

      Payload: an Earth-watching satellite

      Science instruments: Earth camera (sees normal and infrared light)

      Other parts: solar panels, battery, antenna, small thrusters


      TIPS

      - Bigger solar panels give more power, but they add weight.

      - Real mission like yours: NASA''s Landsat.


      Next step: design your mission. Good luck!

      Mission Control'
  - title: Moon Mapper
    subtitle: Hard  |  Fly to the Moon and map its surface
    briefingLines:
    - 'Welcome, astronaut! This is our hardest mission: Moon Mapper.'
    - You will send a spacecraft all the way to the Moon. The Moon is about 384,000 kilometres away!
    - The trip takes about four to five days, so your spacecraft needs lots of fuel.
    - When it arrives, it must fire its engine to slow down. If it is too fast, it will fly right past the Moon!
    - Then it will circle the Moon only about 50 kilometres above the ground, and make a map of the whole surface.
    - Your spacecraft carries a camera to take pictures, and a laser that measures how tall the mountains are and how deep the craters go.
    - Radio messages take more than one second to travel between the Moon and Earth, so your spacecraft must be smart enough to do some work on its own.
    - NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter, called LRO, has been mapping the Moon like this since 2009. Its maps help choose safe landing spots for astronauts.
    - This mission needs the biggest rocket and the most careful planning. Every kilogram counts!
    - Now let's go design your Moon mission. Good luck, astronaut!
    summaryEmail: 'Mission: Moon Mapper

      Difficulty: Hard


      YOUR GOALS

      - Launch on a big rocket and travel about 384,000 km to the Moon

      - Fire your engine to slow down and go into orbit around the Moon

      - Circle the Moon about 50 km above its surface

      - Map the surface with your camera and laser

      - Send your maps back to Earth


      WHAT YOU WILL BUILD

      Rocket: a heavy rocket that can reach the Moon

      Payload: a Moon-orbiting spacecraft

      Science instruments: mapping camera, laser altimeter (measures height)

      Other parts: big fuel tank, main engine, solar panels, battery, large antenna


      TIPS

      - More fuel lets you slow down safely, but it makes the rocket heavier.

      - Real mission like yours: NASA''s Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter (LRO).


      Next step: design your mission. Good luck!

      Mission Control'
  onBriefingFinished:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
